export const KNOWLEDGE_FILES: Record<string, KnowledgeFile> = {
  advice: {
    title: 'Finding mentors and asking for advice',
    body: 'The shape of it. Nobody becomes a mentor because they were asked to be one. Mentors come from a conversation that was worth their time, then a thank-you that showed it landed, then an update a few months later that showed the advice was used. Ask for a conversation, never for mentorship. The people most likely to say yes are alumni, people from the student\'s hometown or club, people two to ten years ahead who remember being where the student is, and anyone whose talk or writing the student can quote back with a real question. Faculty and advisors count too, and they are asked in office hours, not by cold email.\n\nThe first message. Who you are in one line, the real connection, the one question you want their view on (specific enough that they are the right person to answer it), and a fifteen or twenty minute ask with an out. "I\'m deciding between X and Y and you did both" is a question; "any advice you have" is not. Fifty to ninety words. Email for alumni and faculty, LinkedIn when there is no address. No resume, no life story.\n\nThe call. Fifteen to twenty minutes, the student\'s to run. Open with a thirty second version of where you are and what you are deciding. Ask two or three questions you prepared, then follow the conversation. Take notes. Ask what they would do in your position and what they wish they had known. End on time, and close with "is there anyone else you would suggest I talk to". Never ask for a job, an internship or a referral on a first call unless they raise it; a request for help comes later, if at all, and only once the relationship has given something back.\n\nAfter. A thank-you within 24 hours that names one specific thing they said and one thing you will do because of it. Then do it, and tell them in four to eight weeks what happened. That update, with no ask attached, is what turns a call into a relationship; most students skip it and the ones who do not are remembered. Every four to six weeks after that is plenty. When they offer something (an intro, a practice case, a template), take it up with a time within the week.\n\nSwitching fields and non traditional paths. For a student changing direction (nursing to product, teaching to data, audit to a startup), the most useful people are those who made the same switch, found through alumni directories and LinkedIn by searching the old field and the new one together. The question to them is how they told their story, what they did to be credible in the new field, and what they would do differently; the question to a hiring manager in the new field is what they would want to see from someone with the student\'s background.\n\nFreshmen and sophomores. It is early to ask about jobs and exactly right to ask about paths. Three or four conversations a semester with people in fields the student is curious about, each with a thank-you and an update, builds a network before it is needed. Say the year plainly; nobody expects a first-year to have a plan.\n\nWhat never happens: "can you be my mentor" in a first message, a request for the student\'s resume to be reviewed, an hour asked for, a call that runs over, a follow-up that only asks for something, or a mentor\'s advice quoted to them as "you said I should" when they did not. Register: warm, humble, specific, curious, and grateful without flattery.',
  },
  cofounder: {
    title: 'Finding a co-founder',
    body: 'The shape of it. A co-founder is a partner for ten years, chosen in a few months. The best ones come from people the founder has already worked with: a classmate on a hard project, a colleague from an internship, someone from a hackathon team or a club. Next best is someone met through working together on something small first. Matching platforms (YC co-founder matching, campus founder communities, Slack and Discord groups for builders) work when the founder treats the first conversation as the start of a trial, not a decision. The qualities that matter: complementary skills (someone who can build with someone who can sell), shared ambition and pace, honesty under stress, and a problem they both actually care about. Friendship helps and is not enough.\n\nThe first message. Peer to peer, not a pitch and not a job offer. One line on what you are building and why you, one line on why them specifically (what they built, wrote or said), and a twenty minute ask to compare notes on the problem. "I\'m working on X and you spent two years inside the problem at Y; would you be up for twenty minutes to compare notes?" Under eighty words. Never "looking for a technical co-founder" as the subject line, never equity in the first message, never a long vision document attached.\n\nThe conversations. The first call is about the problem and about each other, not about terms. Over the next few weeks, work on something concrete together for two to four weeks: a prototype, customer interviews, a landing page and ten conversations. See how they handle disagreement, deadlines and bad news. Talk explicitly, early, about the things founders fight over: how much time each will commit and when they go full time, money and runway, roles and who decides what, equity, what happens if one wants out, where you will live and work, what success looks like. The YC co-founder questionnaire and the Founder Institute\'s list are good scripts. If a hard conversation is being avoided, that is the answer.\n\nEquity and terms. Equal or near equal splits with four year vesting and a one year cliff are the norm for people starting together; a founder who has been working alone for a year with traction can justify more, and should say so plainly and early. Vesting protects everyone, including the person who stays. Put it in writing with a lawyer or a standard template before incorporating, and before any money comes in.\n\nWho to talk to for advice. Founders who found their co-founder in the way the student is trying, founders who split with one (what they would have asked earlier), and anyone who has worked with the candidate. Ask the candidate for references too; it is normal.\n\nWhat never happens: a co-founder chosen because they were available, equity promised in a first conversation, a "CEO" title handed out before anyone has done anything, a friendship assumed to survive without the hard conversations, or a search run only on a matching platform without doing something together first. Register: peer to peer, direct, candid, respectful of their time and their current commitments.',
  },
  collaboration: {
    title: 'Academic and research collaboration',
    body: 'The shape of it. Researchers answer email about their work from people who have read it. The first message to a professor, a postdoc or a PhD student is about their paper, their method or their dataset, with a precise question or a concrete overlap, never a general request to "work with you" or "learn more about your lab". Undergraduates asking to join a lab are a different, well understood case: the ask is to be a research assistant, and the message shows the student read two papers from the group, took the relevant course, and has time.\n\nThe first message to a researcher. Who you are in one line (year, program, advisor if any). One sentence naming the specific paper or talk and the specific thing in it that matters to your work. One precise question (does the method hold when X, did you try Y, is the dataset available under Z) or one concrete overlap (we have data that tests your claim in a new setting). The ask: an answer by email, or twenty minutes if a call is easier. Attach nothing unless they would want it (a one page summary of your results, a link to a preprint). Eighty to a hundred and twenty words is fine here; precision beats brevity. "Dear Professor Lastname" for faculty you have not met, "Hi Firstname" for PhD students and postdocs. Sign with full name, program and institution.\n\nAsking to join a lab. Email the professor and, if you can find them, the PhD student whose project you would join. Say which project, why it interests you (one specific thing), what you can do (a course, a tool, a prior project), and how many hours a week for how many semesters. Ask whether they take undergraduate research assistants and what they look for in one; ask for a fifteen minute meeting or office hours. Expect silence from half of them and send one polite bump after ten days. Professors read email in bursts; Tuesday to Thursday mornings during term, never the week before a deadline or in August.\n\nThe conversation. Twenty minutes, mostly about their work. Prepare three questions that show you read the paper, one about where the work is going, one about how students take part. For a collaboration, bring one concrete proposal: what you would do, what you would need from them, what each side gets, and a first small step (share a dataset, run one experiment, co-write a workshop paper). Follow up the same day with a short note that restates the step and the date.\n\nWorking together. Agree authorship and credit early and in writing (even an email): who leads, who is an author, what counts as a contribution. Share code and data with a licence and a README. Send short progress updates every two to three weeks with what worked and what did not. Cite them when you use their work, and tell them when you do.\n\nWho to talk to for advice. Graduate students in the field (how they picked an advisor, what the lab is really like), the undergraduate research office, faculty in office hours. The question is about the path and the work, not about a spot.\n\nWhat never happens: a message that could be sent to any lab, "I am passionate about research", a request to be a co-author before doing anything, a question the paper\'s abstract answers, a resume attached to a first email, or more than one bump. Register: precise, academic, curious, specific about the work.',
  },
  community: {
    title: 'Building a community and inviting people in',
    body: "The shape of it. A community is a group of people who keep showing up for each other around something they share: a field, a school, a city, a problem, an identity. The founder's job early is to pick the people, host well, and make it easy to come back. Start smaller than feels right: eight to fifteen people who already like each other's company, a clear reason to gather, a regular rhythm (monthly is enough), and one person who hosts. Growth comes from members inviting people they vouch for, not from a launch.\n\nThe invitation. Specific and personal: a named event or group, when and where, who else is coming (two or three names they would recognise), and why this person in particular would fit or add something. \"We're getting fifteen student founders together on the 12th at the library's upstairs room; Priya and Tom from the hackathon are coming and your work on X is exactly the kind of thing people want to hear about. Would you like to join us?\" Under eighty words. Never a mass BCC, never \"join our community\" with no event behind it, never a Discord link as the whole message. One bump after a week, framed as a reminder with the detail again.\n\nSpeakers and guests. Someone who spoke on campus or wrote something the group read is invited with the specific thing they said and what the group would ask them about; the ask is thirty to forty five minutes, with the format, the audience size and the date in the message. Senior people say yes to a well run room of twenty engaged students far more often than to a coffee chat. Thank them the same day with one thing from the conversation and what the group took from it.\n\nHosting. Start on time, introduce people to each other by what they have in common, give the room one question to work on, end on time, and send a short note afterwards with what happened and when the next one is. Keep a list of members with how they came in and who brought them. Ask members what they want before planning the next three events. The quiet members matter: write to them individually.\n\nPartners and sponsors. A department, a club, a company's university team or a local firm will often give a room, food or a speaker in exchange for being named; ask with the plan, the numbers and the dates in one message, early, and report back afterwards with photos and attendance. A sponsor is told what they get and what they do not (no mailing list, no pitch slot unless agreed).\n\nWho to talk to for advice. People who run communities the student admires (club presidents, meetup organisers, community managers at companies), asked how they got their first twenty members to come back and what they would do differently. Fifteen minutes, with a specific question about a problem the student is having.\n\nWhat never happens: a community announced before it has met twice, members added to a group chat without asking, the same generic invite to a hundred people, an event with no host, a sponsor promised access to members, or a guest invited without the date and the format. Register: friendly, concrete, generous, specific about who and when.",
  },
  consulting: {
    title: 'Management consulting recruiting',
    body: "The timeline: undergraduate recruiting runs a year or more ahead and the deadlines are spread out by firm. For the junior summer internship, some firms close applications in the spring of sophomore year (McKinsey's business analyst intern around March, BCG's summer associate around June), others at the end of the summer before junior year (Bain's associate consultant intern in late August or early September), with interviews through the fall. Full-time applications for seniors close in July to September before senior year, and many full-time seats go to returning interns. [CHANGES YEARLY: check the firm's careers page or a deadline tracker for this year's date before stating one.] Sophomore, first-generation and diversity programs (names change by year) open in the fall and winter and often feed the junior summer class. Some firms recruit on campus at target schools with their own deadlines, so the school's career center calendar matters too. A student networking in the fall and winter of sophomore year is on time for the junior summer; one starting in October of junior year is late for the big three that cycle but fine for a smaller firm or for full-time.\n\nThe process: an online application with a resume and often a cover letter; an online assessment or game at some firms (McKinsey's Solve game, BCG's online case); first round interviews, usually two 30 to 45 minute case interviews with a fit section; then a final round of two or three more cases with partners and principals. A case interview is a business problem solved out loud with the interviewer, judged on structure, numbers, judgment and communication. The fit side matters as much: McKinsey calls its version the PEI (personal experience interview), three deep stories about leadership, drive and influence. Office choice happens at application: the student applies to one or two offices, so \"which office and why\" is a real question to prepare.\n\nWho to talk to: consultants and business analysts one to three years in who share a school, club or hometown; associates and senior associates; an engagement manager or project leader for how staffing and case teams work. A partner only after an event, opening with what they said. Recruiters get a formal, logistics only note with one answerable question. Firms track who a candidate has spoken with and a consultant can flag an application, so the chats matter, but the referral is never asked for in message one.\n\nWhat to ask: how they chose their office and what the first year is like there; how staffing works and how much choice they have; what case prep actually helped and what they would change; what separates the first-years who do well on a case team; how a student from a non-business major should prepare. Never ask a partner about their first year. Never ask about staffing from a banker or a group from a consultant.\n\nRegister: five sentences or fewer, a subject with school and their office or practice (\"Cornell junior, your path to BCG Chicago\"), \"Hi\" and first name for consultants and associates, full name, school and class year in the sign-off. One bump after five to seven business days, then stop. Name the office and the practice, not just the firm. The sequence is chat, thank-you within 24 hours, one or two light touches over two to eight weeks, then \"I'm applying to the Chicago office this fall, would you be comfortable flagging my application when I submit?\" Eight to twelve contacts per firm is normal, expecting around three replies in ten.\n\nCase preparation: start eight to twelve weeks before interviews, learn the frameworks then abandon them, do thirty to fifty live practice cases with partners (classmates, a consulting club, alumni who offer), drill mental math, and prepare three fit stories that can be told in two minutes. Consultants will often offer a practice case on a call; take it up with a time.\n\nOffers: consulting offers usually come with two to four weeks to decide and firms coordinate deadlines less than banks; ask for more time once, politely, with a date.",
  },
  'data-design': {
    title: 'Data science, analytics and design recruiting',
    body: "Data roles. Data scientist, data analyst, machine learning engineer and applied scientist internships post on the tech calendar, August to October for the following summer at large companies, rolling at startups. [CHANGES YEARLY: search the web for a company's current internship posting before stating when it opens.] Titles mean different things at different companies: at some, \"data scientist\" is analytics and SQL and experimentation; at others it is modelling and production code. Research scientist and applied scientist roles usually want a master's or PhD. The process is a resume screen, an online assessment or take-home (SQL, Python, statistics, sometimes a small modelling task), one or two technical screens (probability, A/B testing, SQL on the spot, a case about a metric that moved), and a loop with a behavioural round. Companies want evidence the student can turn a messy question into an analysis that changes a decision: a project with real data and a conclusion, an internship with a number attached, a Kaggle result only as a footnote.\n\nWhat to ask a data person: where their work shows up in product or business decisions; how their time splits between analysis, modelling and building pipelines; what a data intern usually owns over a summer; which tools or skills they had to pick up fastest; what they look for in students who want to do data work on their team.\n\nDesign roles. Product design and UX internships post on a similar calendar at big tech, often a little later (September to November) and with fewer seats; agencies and startups hire closer to the start date. The portfolio is the application: three or four case studies that show the problem, the process, the trade-offs and the shipped result, with the student's own role made clear, hosted on a simple site. A resume without a portfolio link is not read. The process is a portfolio review, a design exercise or take-home, a portfolio presentation to the team, a whiteboard or app critique session, and a behavioural round. Companies want evidence of judgment and of working with engineers and PMs, not visual polish alone.\n\nWhat to ask a designer: how design works with product and engineering on their team; what a design intern owns and how their work is reviewed; what they wish more student portfolios showed; what they learned in the first year that school did not prepare them for; what makes a student portfolio stand out to them.\n\nWho to talk to for both: analysts, scientists or designers one to three years in, last summer's interns, alumni who switched into the field. Managers are asked what they look for and how they scope intern projects so the work ships.\n\nRegister: tech register. \"Hi\" and first name, contractions, five to seven sentences, a subject that says what the email is. Proof beats adjectives: one link to a project or a portfolio. Sign with first name. One bump at five to six days, then stop. LinkedIn works well for designers, who are active there; email for analysts when the address can be found.\n\nPreparation for data: daily SQL and probability practice for six to eight weeks, two or three projects the student can explain end to end including what went wrong, and the statistics behind A/B tests cold. For design: the portfolio first, a practised twenty minute walk-through of the strongest case study, and a critique of the target company's product ready for every conversation.",
  },
  etiquette: {
    title: 'Networking etiquette',
    body: 'The first message asks for insight, never for a job, a referral, a resume review or "any openings". It opens with the real connection (same school, a named referrer, an event they spoke at, a move they made) and carries one line that could only be true of this person. The ask is fifteen minutes, by phone or video, sometime in the next couple of weeks, phrased as a question with an easy out. Fifty to a hundred words. No resume attached to a first message; the one exception is a finance student with a relevant internship who mentions it in half a clause. Never "pick your brain", never "grab coffee sometime", never an hour.\n\nAlumni reply at two to four times the rate of strangers, so alumni come first. Analysts, associates and engineers two to six years ahead reply far more than partners and VPs, and they are the ones who know what the process is like now. A senior person is only written to cold with a hook: they spoke on campus, they wrote something, someone sent you.\n\nA coffee chat is a 15 to 20 minute call that the student runs. Open with a thirty second version of your story, then ask about their path and their team, what they look for, and what they wish they had known. Ask about their work, not about yours. End on time, offering to let them go, and close with "is there anyone else you\'d suggest I talk to". A referral or an internship is never raised on the first call unless they bring it up. One chat per person before an interview invite; ask for a pointer to one more person instead of a second chat.\n\nThe thank-you goes out within 24 hours. It names when you spoke, one specific thing they said in their words, one thing you did or will do because of it, and asks whether it is alright to send a question later. Two people at the same firm never get the same text; they compare. Whoever introduced you is thanked the day it happens and told how the conversation went afterwards. That report back is the most skipped step and the one people remember.\n\nSilence is normal. A quarter to a half of first messages get a reply, more from alumni, fewer from strangers. One bump after five to eight business days by email (ten to twelve on LinkedIn), two sentences, "in case it got buried", the ask in one clause, an out or a pointer to someone else. In finance a second bump a week later is fine; in consulting and tech one is enough. Never more than three unanswered messages to one person. After that let it rest and try someone else on the team.\n\nAn intro goes through a double opt-in. Ask the connector first with a two line forwardable blurb they can send as is: who you are, why this person, the specific ask, an out. The connector checks the other side is willing before connecting anyone. Never ask them to vouch for things they do not know.\n\nOnce a reply comes, answer the same day, within two business days at the outside. Propose two concrete windows with the time zone plus "or send me a time". Confirm the slot, the zone, the format and who calls whom, then send the invite. If they ask for something (a resume, which teams), answer that before anything else. Reschedule once, apologise once, offer two replacements.\n\nChannels: email is better for alumni and anyone whose address you can find, LinkedIn for a first touch when you cannot and for people who are active there. Never the same message on both at once. A LinkedIn connection note is at most 300 characters (free accounts cut at 200) and must carry the ask itself; never a bare request and never "can I ask you something". When a LinkedIn reply comes, move to email to schedule. Orbit never sends a LinkedIn message itself; the student copies it over.\n\nSend windows, finance and consulting: Tuesday to Thursday, mid morning in the recipient\'s time zone; avoid Monday 9am and Friday afternoon. Tech: within three days of a posting beats any window. Stay in touch every four to six weeks after a conversation with one real update tied to something they said and "no reply needed".\n\nWords that mark a message as mail merge, never used: "I hope this finds you well", "reach out", "pick your brain", "leverage", "passionate about", "impressed by your background", "any advice you have", "touch base", "circle back".',
  },
  'finance-other': {
    title:
      'Finance beyond banking: private equity, equity research, sales and trading, asset management, corporate finance',
    body: 'Private equity. Buyout and growth funds hire almost entirely from investment banking analysts after one to two years, through a headhunter driven process that now starts within months of an analyst\'s first day ("on cycle"), plus a smaller "off cycle" market later. A handful of large funds (Blackstone, KKR, Apollo, Carlyle, TPG, Bain Capital and a few others) run undergraduate analyst and summer analyst programs that recruit on the banking calendar, sophomore year, with applications in the late summer and fall. [CHANGES YEARLY: search the web for the fund\'s current program before stating when it opens.] For most students the honest answer is banking first, then PE. A student who wants PE asks a PE person how they made the move, what surprised them about the work, and how they would prepare for buy side recruiting as a student; someone senior is asked what they look for in the analysts they hire and what they would tell a student who wants to end up in private equity.\n\nEquity research and sales and trading. Both sit inside banks and recruit on the bank\'s calendar, often through the same application portal as banking with a separate division choice, sometimes slightly later. Research wants writing, accounting and a view on a stock; a student should have one or two stock pitches ready. Sales and trading wants markets interest, quick numbers and a read on people; a student should follow markets daily and know why things moved this week. Both are smaller than banking and have shrunk over the years, so networking with people on the desk or in the coverage group matters more, and the question is about their desk or sector, never "your group" in the banking sense.\n\nAsset management and hedge funds. Large asset managers (Fidelity, BlackRock, Vanguard, T. Rowe, Wellington, Capital Group, PIMCO) run summer analyst programs that post in the late summer and fall of the year before; hedge funds rarely take undergraduates except a few large multi-manager platforms with structured programs and the quant firms (see quant). A stock pitch or a view on a market is the currency in every conversation.\n\nCorporate finance, FP&A, wealth management, commercial and corporate banking, insurance and fintech. Larger, later and more forgiving timelines: many post in the fall and spring of the year before and recruit on campus. These are good paths for students who are late for banking or who want finance with better hours, and the conversation should treat them as real choices, not fallbacks.\n\nWho to talk to across these: analysts and associates one to three years in, alumni, people who made the move the student wants to make. Senior people only with a hook. Recruiters for logistics only.\n\nRegister: finance register. Five sentences or fewer, a subject with school, class year and their desk, sector or fund, "Hi" and first name for analysts and associates, "Mr. or Ms. Lastname" for senior people not yet met. Full name, school and class year in the sign-off. Up to two bumps a week apart in banking adjacent roles, one elsewhere. Email from the .edu address, Tuesday to Thursday mid morning.',
  },
  fundraising: {
    title: 'Raising a first round',
    body: 'Rounds. Pre-seed is the first outside money: a few hundred thousand to around two million dollars from angels, pre-seed funds and accelerators, almost always on SAFEs. Seed is the first institutional round, typically two to five million dollars led by a seed fund, on SAFEs or as a priced round with a lead who sets the terms. Series A is five to twenty million dollars from a venture firm on a priced round once there is real traction. [CHANGES YEARLY: round sizes and caps drift; quote a current benchmark from the web, never a number from memory.] A SAFE (simple agreement for future equity) converts into shares at the next priced round at a valuation cap, sometimes with a discount; the post-money SAFE is the standard. A priced round sells preferred shares at an agreed valuation with a term sheet, a lead investor and legal costs. Pro rata is the right to keep a percentage in later rounds. Founders usually give up fifteen to twenty five percent across pre-seed and seed together.\n\nHow a round works. A lead sets terms, takes the largest share and signals to everyone else; angels and smaller funds fill the rest. Many seed rounds are a stack of SAFEs with no formal lead. Raise in a tight window: line up conversations, take first meetings in the same two or three weeks so investors move in parallel and feel time pressure, and never say who else is in until it is signed. Ask for the amount that buys eighteen to twenty four months of runway to the milestone the next round needs, and know that number cold.\n\nWho to talk to and how. Warm intros beat cold notes by an order of magnitude, and the best warm intro is from a founder the investor backed. Portfolio founders, other founders, angels, former colleagues and alumni investors are the routes, in that order. A cold note can work with a seed fund that says it takes them: two lines on what the company does and why now, one line of traction, a clear ask ("would you be open to a look"), and the deck or a one pager attached or linked. The forwardable blurb is the unit of a fundraise: two or three sentences a connector can send as is. Investors are checked before they are pitched: stage, cheque size, sectors, recent deals, whether they lead. Pitching a Series B fund on a pre-seed wastes both sides\' time.\n\nThe call. A first call is usually thirty minutes with a partner at a small fund, or an associate or principal who screens at a larger one. Associates and analysts do not decide; they champion. Decisions happen at a partner meeting, often weekly. The investor wants the problem, why now, the insight, traction, the team, the market, and the round: how much, on what terms, who is leading, how much is committed. Answer questions straight, say what you do not know, and ask about their process and timing at the end. Afterwards, send a short note the same day with the deck and any data room link, then keep every investor who passed or paused on a monthly update: metrics, wins, asks. Many seed cheques come from an update six months after a no.\n\nWhat never goes in the first message: a long pitch, the full deck pasted in, "disruptive", a valuation, a deadline that is not real, and anything about the student\'s recruiting. The register is crisp, numbers first, confident, short.',
  },
  glossary: {
    title: 'Glossary',
    body: "Recruiting. Superday: a bank's final round, several back to back interviews in one day, technical and behavioural. HireVue: a recorded video interview with timed answers, used as an early screen by banks and some companies. OA (online assessment): a timed test early in a tech, quant or consulting process; coding for engineers, probability and mental math for quant, a game or case for consulting. Summer analyst: a bank or asset manager's junior summer intern. Return offer: the full time offer given to a summer intern at the end of the summer. Off-cycle: an internship in the fall or spring rather than the summer, or recruiting outside the main calendar. Bulge bracket: the largest global banks (Goldman Sachs, JPMorgan, Morgan Stanley, Bank of America, Citi, Barclays, UBS, Deutsche Bank). Elite boutique: smaller advisory banks with large deals and no lending (Evercore, Lazard, Centerview, Moelis, PJT, Perella Weinberg, Qatalyst). Middle market: banks that advise on smaller deals (Houlihan Lokey, Jefferies, William Blair, Baird, Piper Sandler). Group: a bank's industry or product team (TMT, healthcare, FIG, M&A, leveraged finance); some banks place summer analysts by group. Coverage: the industry group that covers a set of companies. PEI: McKinsey's personal experience interview, the fit part of its interviews, three deep stories. Case interview: a business problem solved aloud with the interviewer, the core of consulting interviews. Fit interview: the behavioural half of an interview. MBB: McKinsey, Bain and BCG. Big Four: Deloitte, PwC, EY, KPMG. Target school: a school a firm recruits at on campus; non-target: one it does not. Networking call or coffee chat: a fifteen to twenty minute conversation about someone's path, the unit of recruiting networking. Informational interview: the formal name for the same thing. Referral: a current employee flagging a candidate to recruiting, which moves the application out of the pile. Req or requisition: a posting's internal ID. Exploding offer: an offer with a short deadline. Renege: accepting an offer and then backing out, which burns bridges. Target function: the kind of job the student is going for. Cycle: one recruiting season.\n\nInvesting jobs. Sourcing: finding companies to invest in. Diligence: checking a company before investing. Memo: the written case for an investment. Scout: a person, often a student or founder, who refers deals to a fund for a small cheque book or a bonus. Platform: a fund's non investing staff (community, talent, marketing). Analyst and associate: the junior investing roles; principal: senior, often leads deals but is not a partner; partner (GP, general partner): decides and sits on boards.\n\nFundraising. Pre-seed, seed, Series A: the first three rounds, from a few hundred thousand to tens of millions. SAFE: a simple agreement for future equity, money now for shares at the next priced round; post-money SAFE: the standard form, whose cap includes the SAFE money. Valuation cap: the maximum valuation at which a SAFE converts. Discount: a percentage off the next round's price for SAFE holders. Priced round: shares sold at an agreed valuation with a term sheet. Lead: the investor who sets terms and takes the largest share. Term sheet: the one page summary of the deal before legal documents. Pro rata: the right to invest in later rounds to keep your percentage. Dilution: the share of the company founders give up. Runway: months of cash left at the current burn. Burn: cash spent per month. Partner meeting: where a fund decides, usually weekly. Data room: a folder of documents investors review. Investor update: the monthly email to current and prospective investors. Angel: an individual who invests their own money. Accelerator: a program that invests a small amount in a cohort of companies (Y Combinator, Techstars). Carry: the share of profits a fund's partners earn. Cap table: who owns what. Vesting: shares earned over time, typically four years with a one year cliff.",
  },
  hiring: {
    title: 'Hiring your first people',
    body: 'The shape of it. Early hires come from the founders\' network and from people the founders write to directly, not from job boards. The first ten hires decide the company; each one is worth twenty hours of the founders\' time. Write the role down first: what this person will own in the first ninety days, what they must have done before, what you will pay (a range, plus equity), and why someone good would leave a good job for this. If that last line is not convincing, fix the pitch before writing to anyone.\n\nSourcing. Start with the network: former colleagues, classmates, people met at events, people the current team worked with, the investors\' networks, customers. Then passive candidates: people in similar roles at companies one stage ahead, people who shipped something relevant in public, people who commented thoughtfully on your space. A referral from someone the candidate trusts converts far better than a cold note, so ask the team and the investors by name for introductions, with a forwardable blurb about the role.\n\nThe first message to a candidate. Short, specific and respectful of their current job. Line one is why them: something they built, wrote or did, not a compliment on their background. Line two is the company and the role in plain words: what you do, the stage, and the one thing that makes this role interesting (the problem, the ownership, the team). Line three is a low pressure ask: "open to hearing about it over a call this week or next?" Never "I came across your profile", never "exciting opportunity", never a job description pasted in, never the salary left as a mystery if they ask. Under a hundred words. One bump after a week, then leave it; people change their minds in six months, so keep a list.\n\nThe process. Keep it short and decided: an intro call with a founder (thirty minutes, sell as much as screen), a work sample or paid project that looks like the real job, a conversation with the team, references that you actually call (ask who else to speak to, and ask every reference "would you hire them again"). Decide within a week of the last conversation and say so. Good candidates have other options; the fastest honest process wins. Tell every candidate who does not get the role, kindly and promptly.\n\nThe offer. Base, equity (shares and percentage, vesting, strike price, what the last round valued the company at), start date, and the reasons in plain words. Walk them through the equity; most candidates do not know how to read it and will trust whoever explains it honestly. Give a week to decide, be available for questions, and let them talk to the team and an investor if they want. If they say no, ask why and stay in touch.\n\nWho to talk to for advice. Founders a stage ahead who made the hire you are making, the hiring manager at a company you admire, a recruiter friend for market comp. The ask is fifteen minutes on how they found and closed their first engineer or salesperson, not for candidates.\n\nWhat never happens: a message that reads as a blast, a title inflated to get a yes, an offer that cannot be honoured, poaching from a friend\'s company without a word to the friend, or a candidate left waiting without an update. Register: direct, warm, specific, respectful of where they are now.',
  },
  ib: {
    title: 'Investment banking recruiting',
    body: 'The timeline runs early, about 18 to 21 months before the internship, and it has kept moving earlier. Summer analyst recruiting for the junior summer happens during sophomore year: elite boutiques (Evercore, Moelis, PWP, Lazard, Centerview and the like) open applications in September to November of sophomore year, bulge brackets (Goldman, JPMorgan, Morgan Stanley, Citi, Bank of America, Barclays, UBS) in December and January, with a trickle of middle market and regional banks into the spring. Applications are rolling, interviews start within weeks of a posting, and most seats are filled by late spring of sophomore year. [CHANGES YEARLY: search the web for a bank\'s current application open date before stating one; "Summer 2028 investment banking applications" finds the trackers.] So the networking window is the spring of freshman year through the fall of sophomore year, two to four months before each bank opens. A sophomore in October is on time but must move now; a junior writing about a summer internship is late: the right ask then is about off-cycle programs, smaller banks and boutiques that recruit closer to the summer, or the full-time path, and the question to a banker becomes "how you would approach recruiting from here if you were me", never "before recruiting starts".\n\nSophomore insight, "fast track" and diversity programs (names change: look them up) open in the late summer and fall of freshman and sophomore year, with deadlines in October, and often convert straight into junior summer interviews. Full-time recruiting outside of return offers is small; most full-time analysts come from the summer class, so the summer analyst seat is the goal. Off-cycle internships (fall or spring) exist at some banks and many boutiques and are a second door.\n\nThe process: an online application, often a HireVue (a recorded video interview with timed answers), first round calls with analysts and associates, then a superday: several back to back 30 minute interviews in one day with a mix of technical questions (accounting, valuation, the three statements, DCF, LBO basics) and behavioural ones ("walk me through your resume", "why banking", "why us", a deal you followed). Networking is what gets a resume pulled from the pile at most banks: referrals from analysts and associates in a group get looked at, and some groups effectively hire the people they have talked to.\n\nWho to talk to: analysts and associates, alumni first, then people from your club, hometown or a shared employer. Managing directors and VPs are written to only with a hook. Recruiters and campus teams are for logistics questions only (deadlines, which group to apply to), in a formal note, one question. Banks hire by group at some firms (the student applies to a group, say Healthcare or TMT) and generalist at others; asking an analyst how their group works and how placement happens is a good question.\n\nWhat to ask on a call: how they chose their group or their bank and what they wish they had known going in; what the first year looks like and how staffing works; what separates the summer analysts who do well; what to know before the superday. Never ask for a referral, an internship, "openings" or a resume review in a first message, and never whether the group "still has seats". A thank-you within 24 hours names one thing they said; analysts in a group compare notes, so no two get the same text.\n\nRegister: five sentences or fewer, a subject with school, class year and their group ("Michigan \'28, quick question on Evercore healthcare"), "Hi" and first name for analysts and associates (they are two to four years older and read "Mr." as a culture misfit), "Mr. or Ms. Lastname" for VPs and above you have not met. Sign with full name, school and class year. Email from the .edu address Tuesday to Thursday, mid morning their time. Two bumps allowed a week apart, then move on. Volume is real in this sector: ten to twenty first messages a day in the networking window is normal, a few hundred people over a cycle from a target school and more from a non-target.\n\nInternational students: most large US banks sponsor work visas for full-time analysts and take international students for summer roles on CPT; some boutiques and smaller firms do not. Sponsorship goes on the application when asked and never in a first networking message; ask about it on a call with an analyst in the same situation or with the recruiter late in the process.\n\nOffers: an exploding offer is a real deadline, but most banks give a week or two more when asked promptly and politely, especially when another process is close to deciding. Ask the recruiter the same day by phone or email, say you are excited, and name the date you can decide by. Never accept and keep looking; a reneged offer burns the school and the student.',
  },
  pm: {
    title: 'Product management recruiting',
    body: 'The shape of the market: entry level PM roles are rare. A few dozen companies run APM (associate product manager) or RPM programs that take new grads, each with twenty to fifty seats and thousands of applicants, and they open in a short window, usually August to October of senior year for the following summer, sometimes in the spring. [CHANGES YEARLY: search the web for the program\'s current application window before stating one.] PM internships exist at big tech and some larger startups and post on the same calendar as engineering internships, August to October. Most people who end up in product get there sideways: engineering, design, consulting, a rotational program, a startup where they wore the hat, or an internal move after a year or two in another function. A student who wants product should say so, and also have a credible plan B route and be honest with themselves about it.\n\nThe process: a resume screen, a recruiter call, a product sense interview (design a product for a user, improve a feature, prioritise), an analytical or execution interview (metrics, a drop in a number, trade-offs), sometimes a technical interview (lightweight: how a system works, an API, SQL) and a behavioural round. Companies want evidence of shipping something and of judgment: a project with users, a club product, an internship where the student owned a decision. A portfolio or a short case write-up helps more than a cover letter.\n\nWho to talk to: APMs and PMs one to three years in, interns from last summer, alumni who moved from engineering or consulting into product. A group PM or director is asked what they look for, not what a PM intern does. Recruiters for APM programs are worth one logistics question once the program is open.\n\nWhat to ask: how their team decides what to build next and who makes the final call; what a PM intern or APM actually owns over a summer or a first year; what they leaned on most from their background when they started; what separates a good product sense answer from a great one in their interviews; whether they would recommend engineering or a startup first for someone who wants product. Never "how do I become a PM" as the opener; the student has read the basics and asks the one thing this person can answer.\n\nRegister: tech register. "Hi" and first name, contractions, five to seven sentences, a subject that says what the email is, proof over adjectives (a product the student built or a write-up on the company\'s product). Sign with first name. One bump at five to six days, then stop. LinkedIn works well; PMs are active there.\n\nPreparation: a month or two of structured practice on product sense and execution questions with a peer, a framework the student actually believes rather than a memorised one, three stories about products they shipped with a number in each, and a point of view on the company\'s product (one thing they would change and why) ready for every interview and every coffee chat.',
  },
  quant: {
    title: 'Quantitative trading and research recruiting',
    body: 'The timeline: quant trading and quant research internships recruit early and fast. Firms post summer internships for the following year in July to September and interview in the early fall; many offers are out by October and November, with a smaller second wave in the winter. [CHANGES YEARLY: search the web for the firm\'s current posting before stating when it opens.] Sophomore and first-year programs exist at several firms (names change) and are a real door. Full-time roles mostly go to returning interns, so the internship matters. Applications go straight through the careers page; referrals help at the resume stage but the process is mostly the tests.\n\nThe roles: quant trader (pricing, risk, decisions under time pressure, markets intuition), quant researcher (statistics, modelling, signal research, usually a strong math or CS background, often a master\'s or PhD for research roles at some firms), software engineer (systems, low latency, infrastructure), and quant developer in between. The student should know which they are applying for; at many firms trading and research are separate applications and the interviews differ.\n\nThe process: an online assessment (probability, mental math, pattern questions, sometimes coding), one or two phone or video rounds (probability and expected value, brainteasers, market making games, statistics, for research a programming or modelling question), then a final round of three to five interviews, often on site, including a behavioural conversation. Firms want fast correct reasoning under uncertainty, comfort with being wrong out loud, and genuine interest in markets or in the math. Finance knowledge is not expected; probability and clear thinking are.\n\nWho to talk to: traders and researchers one to three years in, last summer\'s interns, alumni. A desk head or a partner answers one question by email and only one they alone can answer; nobody senior is asked "what separates the interns who get return offers" by a student with no internship there.\n\nWhat to ask: how they prepared for the interviews and what the first months were like; how they decided between trading and research; what a typical research problem looks like in the first year; what separates the new hires who do well on their desk; for a computer science student writing to someone senior, what gap they see most often in candidates coming from CS rather than math or statistics. Never "the office" (a quant firm has desks, not offices) and never "your group" (that is a banking word).\n\nRegister: short and direct, "Hi" and first name, four or five sentences, a subject that names the firm and the question. Email from the .edu address. Sign with first name, school and class year. One bump after a week, then stop. These firms are small and remember people, so one good note beats three.\n\nPreparation: eight to twelve weeks of probability and expected value drills, mental math daily, the standard interview prep books worked through twice, market making and betting games practised out loud with a friend, and for research roles one or two modelling projects the student can defend. A genuine interest the student can talk about for ten minutes (a market, a game, a statistical puzzle) is what the behavioural round looks for.',
  },
  sales: {
    title: 'Selling to your first customers',
    body: 'The shape of it. Early sales is founder led: the person who built the thing talks to the people who have the problem. The goal of a first message is a conversation, not a sale. Pick a narrow segment (one kind of company, one role) where the pain is sharp and the buyer can decide, and learn it before writing to it. A buyer is researched before they are written to: their role, what their team does, a trigger (a new role, a funding round, a hiring post, a product launch, a public complaint, a regulation) that makes now the moment.\n\nThe first message. Two to four short lines, under eighty words. Line one is the trigger or the specific observation about them, so they know it is not a blast. Line two is what you do in plain words, for people like them, with one concrete result if you have one ("three clinics cut no-shows by a third"). Line three is the ask: a fifteen or twenty minute call, phrased as a question with an out ("worth a quick look?"). No feature list, no attachment, no "I hope this finds you well", no "revolutionary". The subject is two to five plain words about them, never about you. Send from a real name and address. Fifteen to thirty of these a day, each one individually written, beats a hundred from a tool.\n\nFollow-up. Sales is the one place where several follow-ups are normal: a bump after three or four business days, another a week later, a last one a week after that, each adding one new thing (a result, a short case, a question), never guilt. Then stop and come back in a quarter with news. A no is information; ask what would have to be true.\n\nThe discovery call. Twenty to thirty minutes. Ask before you show: what they do today, what breaks, what it costs them, who else cares, what they have tried. Show the product for ten minutes against what they said, not a generic demo. End with a concrete next step and a date: a pilot, a second call with the decision maker, a trial. Follow up the same day with a short recap of what they said and the next step.\n\nPricing and pilots. Charge from the first customer, even a little; free pilots teach you nothing about whether they will pay. A paid pilot with a clear success metric and a date to decide is the standard early deal. Know the budget owner and the procurement path at anything larger than a small business.\n\nWho to talk to. The person whose week your product changes, and the person who can sign: often two people. In a small business they are the same; in a mid sized company write to the operator first and let them bring the buyer. Warm routes (a customer who refers you, an investor\'s portfolio, an alum at the company) convert far better than cold, and a referral from a happy customer is the best message you will ever send.\n\nWhat never happens: the same message on email and LinkedIn at once, a pitch to someone who said no last month, a claim about a customer who has not agreed to be named, a discount offered before they asked, more than three unanswered touches in a row, or any line that could be sent to any company. Register: short, concrete, specific, no hype, respectful of their time.',
  },
  tech: {
    title: 'Software engineering recruiting',
    body: 'The timeline: big tech and most large companies post summer internships for the following year in August to October and hire on a rolling basis until the seats are gone, often by December; some open earlier (July) and some post into the spring. [CHANGES YEARLY: search the web for a company\'s current internship posting before stating when it opens.] New grad roles follow the same calendar. Startups hire year round, usually two to three months before they need someone, and many never post an internship until someone asks. Return offers from the summer fill a large share of new grad seats, so the internship is the goal. Timing beats polish: an application within a few days of a posting is read; one a month later often is not.\n\nThe process: an online application, often an online assessment (a timed coding test, a HackerRank or CodeSignal), one or two technical phone screens (data structures and algorithms, sometimes a practical coding exercise), then a virtual or onsite loop of three to five interviews: coding, sometimes system design for new grads, and a behavioural round. Some companies run a team match after the offer. Big tech interview on fundamentals; startups interview on what you have built and whether you can ship. Referrals move an application from the portal pile to a recruiter\'s queue at most large companies and are easy to give; nobody minds being asked by someone they have talked to once.\n\nWho to talk to: a recently joined engineer or a peer individual contributor on a team you would join, an intern from last summer, an alum two or three years out. Not the VP and not a recruiter who does not own the posting. Messaging several people at one company individually is fine; never say so. Hiring managers are only written to cold with proof: one clickable artifact built on their product or in their domain.\n\nWhat to ask: what their team owns and what the stack looks like day to day; how intern projects get picked and how much ships; what the strongest interns did differently in their first month; what to practise before interviews there. A senior engineer or manager is asked what they look for in interns and new grads, or for a pointer to someone on the team closer to the intern class. Proof beats adjectives: one deployed app, a repo or a PDF, zero sentences about passion.\n\nReferrals: ask before applying through the portal (many applicant systems cannot attach a referral afterwards), with the two minute package: the exact role and requisition ID with a link, the resume, one line of fit, and an out. An alum, a mutual contact or someone who posted that their team is hiring can be asked directly; a stranger gets a question first.\n\nRegister: "Hi" and first name, contractions, five to seven sentences, a subject that says what the email is ("Backend intern application, built a rate limiter on your API"). Sign with first name only. One bump at five to six days, then stop for good. LinkedIn works well in tech; cold email to an engineer gets roughly one reply in ten, a referred application roughly one interview in four, a portal application one or two in a hundred.\n\nInterview preparation: two to three months of daily practice on the common problem patterns (arrays and hashing, two pointers, trees, graphs, dynamic programming), talking out loud while solving, mock interviews with peers, and a one minute story for each project on the resume with a number in it. For startups, be ready to walk through something you built end to end and to say what you would do differently.\n\nOffers: tech offers often come with one to three weeks and companies will usually extend once when another process is near a decision; competing offers can be negotiated for new grads at large companies, politely and with a number.',
  },
  'vc-job': {
    title: 'Getting a job in venture capital',
    body: "The shape of the market: venture firms are small. A typical seed or Series A fund has five to twenty investment staff and hires an analyst or associate every year or two, often without posting the role. There is no campus recruiting cycle; a handful of firms run analyst programs or summer internships with applications usually in the winter and spring, and some larger platforms run structured programs. [CHANGES YEARLY: search the web for a firm's current analyst program or fellowship before stating when it opens.] Most junior investors come from one of three routes: two years in banking or consulting, a few years operating at a startup (growth, product, engineering), or a student fellowship and scout network that turns into a role. Being known to the firm before a seat opens is how most seats get filled.\n\nEntry roles: analyst (sourcing, research, memos, data), associate (sourcing plus diligence and portfolio support, often after banking or an MBA), and on the platform side community, talent or content roles that can lead to investing. Student programs: campus scout programs (a small cheque book or a referral bonus for finding founders at your school), fellowships that run a semester or a summer with a cohort, and university venture funds run by students. Sourcing is the junior job: finding founders early, keeping a list, writing crisp notes. A student with a sourcing track record (founders they know, a newsletter, a campus fund) is far more interesting than one with a finance resume and nothing else.\n\nThe process: an introduction or a cold note that lands because of a point of view, a few conversations with the team, often a take-home (source three companies in a space and write why, or diligence a company and write an investment memo), case questions on a startup's model and market, and a conversation with the partners. Firms want judgment, taste, hustle and writing. The interviews are about the student's view: a sector they know, a company they would back and why, a company the firm backed and what they would have asked.\n\nWho to talk to: analysts and associates (how they got in, what their week actually looks like, what they own on a deal), principals and partners only with a point of view ready and usually through a warm route. Founders the firm backed are a good side door: they know the partners and how they decide.\n\nWhat to ask: how they got into venture and what their week looks like; how they source and what a first meeting with a founder looks like; what junior people at their firm own on a deal; whether they would recommend operating first; how to prepare for venture recruiting now; what they look for in people early in their careers who want to invest. Never \"do you have any openings\" and never a pitch for the student's own startup in a note that asks about a job.\n\nRegister: short, direct, a view in the first two sentences. Email or LinkedIn both work; investors live in both. \"Hi\" and first name. Five sentences, one specific thing about a company they backed or a post they wrote, one ask of fifteen minutes. One bump after a week, then stop. A thank-you with one new idea or company in it is remembered.\n\nPreparation: pick two sectors and know twenty companies in each, write short memos on three startups the student would back, follow the firm's recent investments and have a view on them, and build a habit of meeting founders on campus. Writing in public (a newsletter, a few posts with real analysis) is the most reliable signal a student can create.",
  },
};
